commit e2cda67079381b8e048754aeb96b77de492a3bea
Author: Your Name <you@example.com>
Date:   Thu Oct 29 20:14:37 2026 +0200

    Implement runtime TLS interception and DOM engine switching, with close-delimited response fix

    - Runtime TLS interception switch (GET/PUT /api/proxy/interception): a process started
      without -intercept-tls can now be switched to intercepting and back without a restart.
      On attaches the CA (loaded from or generated into -ca-dir), off detaches it while
      keeping the CA so clients that trust it keep trusting it. All TlsPolicy engine reads
      go through the new locked Engine() accessor instead of touching the field directly,
      since the engine is swapped at runtime (same race-free pattern as the CA fields).

    - Runtime DOM engine switch (GET/PUT /api/scanner/dom): the -dom flag was read once at
      startup, so mid-engagement a process that started without it ran every DOM check as
      capability-less until restart. The scanners now hold one SwitchableBrowsers whose
      engine the console can set, change or remove at runtime; setting one switches the DOM
      families on (EnableDOMChecks), removing it leaves the switch alone so the operator's
      chosen families are not silently overwritten, and the old engine is closed after the
      swap (outside the lock, so a slow Chrome shutdown never blocks a check).

    - -upstream-tls-verify flag: overrides the saved network TLS-verify setting for this run,
      routed through the sender's own document and the proxy's TLS policy so the console and
      repeater see the same decision. Defaults true to preserve the existing behaviour.

    - Exchange restore fix: the wtov pointer conversion made Exchange.Request a pointer but
      dropped the LoadExchanges assignment, so after a restart every exchange came back with
      no request — search found nothing in any request captured before a restart.

    - DOM AST scope cycle fix: walking a self-reentrant closure (jQuery-style el.each calls $
      from inside a function literal $ defines) used to re-parent the cached scope onto its
      own chain, and lookup then looped forever on the cycle, holding the proxy's client
      socket open until their timeout. A nested activation now gets its own scope.

    - Close-delimited response handling: a response that ends by connection close (no
      Content-Length / chunked) now hands the client its EOF (half-close over TLS, or a
      CloseWrite) before the record runs — passive scan, DOM pass, page observer — instead
      of after it. A slow record held the client waiting for an answer it already had.

    - Route manifest additions and test coverage: /api/proxy/interception and /api/scanner/dom
      registered in the route manifest; json_nil_slice_test gains their 503 plain-text entries
      and the /api/issues/ catch-all case.

    Binaries, local git hooks and the beads_migrtion scratch repo are deliberately excluded.

